# WA Connector WhatsApp bridge — environment template. Copy to .env for local
# dev (dist/index.js does NOT load .env itself; use `tsx --env-file=.env` or
# Railway's variables UI in production).

# --- Supabase (same project as the web app) --------------------------------
SUPABASE_URL=https://YOUR-PROJECT.supabase.co
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=

# --- Credential encryption (hard rule 1) -----------------------------------
# 32 random bytes, base64. Generate with:
#   node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64'))"
# This key exists ONLY here (Railway env). Losing it = every client re-scans
# a QR (annoying, recoverable). Leaking it + a Supabase breach = session
# hijack — treat like the Supabase service key itself.
WA_CREDS_KEY=
# Optional decrypt-only previous key, set during rotation (see README.md
# "Key rotation"). Remove once every row has been re-encrypted.
WA_CREDS_KEY_PREVIOUS=

# --- Bridge API auth (hard rule 2) ------------------------------------------
# Shared secret the portal presents as `Authorization: Bearer …`. Generate the
# same way as WA_CREDS_KEY. Must match the portal's WHATSAPP_BRIDGE_SECRET.
WA_BRIDGE_SECRET=

# --- HTTP -------------------------------------------------------------------
PORT=8080

# --- Pacing (hard rule 4 — configuration, not magic numbers) ----------------
# Minimum gap between two sends on ONE linked number, plus uniform random
# jitter on top. Defaults: 45s + 0-30s. A linked business number gets
# banned for machine-gun sending; do not lower these without a reason.
WA_SEND_MIN_GAP_MS=45000
WA_SEND_JITTER_MS=30000
# Max sends per linked number per UTC day. WhatsApp tolerates far more from an aged
# number, but sequences are follow-ups, not blasts — 150 is deliberate.
WA_DAILY_CAP=150
# Typing simulation (src/typing.ts): show "typing..." in the recipient's chat
# before each outbound TEXT part, for 1s + 30ms per character (+/-20%),
# capped at WA_TYPING_MAX_MS. Sending with no composing state first is a bot
# signature. Best effort: a failed presence update never fails the send.
WA_TYPING_INDICATOR_ENABLED=true
WA_TYPING_MAX_MS=4000

# --- Session lease (split-brain guard) --------------------------------------
# Heartbeat interval and the staleness TTL a rival instance may steal at.
# TTL must be comfortably > heartbeat (default 3x — a holder is declared
# dead only after missing 3 consecutive beats). The resume-sweep retry loop
# also runs on WA_LOCK_HEARTBEAT_MS, so a new deploy takes over a session
# within ~one heartbeat of the old instance releasing (SIGTERM) or ~TTL of
# it going dark (frozen/crashed/killed before its SIGTERM handler could run).
# Was 30000/90000 until an incident where a dead container's leases sat held
# for the full 90s TTL, per session, before the replacement could take over
# (multi-minute recovery) — see src/config.ts's `lockTtlMs` JSDoc for the
# full writeup. 10000/30000 keeps the same 3x ratio at a third of the stall.
WA_LOCK_HEARTBEAT_MS=10000
WA_LOCK_TTL_MS=30000

# Ceiling (ms) a graceful shutdown waits for a session's in-flight Signal
# key/creds writes to settle before releasing that session's lease — the
# fix for the ratchet-desync incident where a killed-mid-persist key write
# left the replacement container's ratchet behind the real one (every
# client's OWN phone then shows "Waiting for this message", `Bad MAC` in
# logs). Sessions now shut down CONCURRENTLY (src/sessions.ts's
# `shutdown()`), so this only needs margin for ONE session's flush plus its
# `lock.release()` round trip, not the sum across every session on the box.
# Must stay under 6000 — src/main.ts's hard stop fires at 10s past SIGTERM, and
# a flush timing out is CORRELATED with Supabase being slow, i.e. exactly
# when that release round trip is also slowest; `loadConfig` refuses to boot
# above that ceiling. Must also be at least 500 — anything lower and
# flush()'s budgetMs <= 0 check (src/pending-writes.ts) can return
# `timedOut: true` without awaiting a single write, silently reverting to
# the pre-fix incident behaviour while this var LOOKS configured;
# `loadConfig` refuses to boot below that floor too.
WA_SHUTDOWN_FLUSH_TIMEOUT_MS=5000

# Max automatic reconnect attempts for a session that has NEVER completed
# pairing (still stuck at 'waiting_qr' — nobody has scanned yet). Without a
# cap an unscanned QR re-registers with WhatsApp roughly every reconnect
# cycle forever; after this many attempts the bridge gives up and requires
# an explicit /connect to try again.
WA_MAX_QR_RECONNECT_ATTEMPTS=5

# --- Inbound reply poke (Batch 1 of the reply/auto-stop/trigger package) ---
# and the GHL provider Phase 2 outbound-status poke (docs/GHL-PROVIDER-PLAN.md,
# file list #16) — THREE knobs, ONE shared secret. PORTAL_INBOUND_URL and
# PORTAL_OUTBOUND_URL are each independently optional (either, both, or
# neither), but WA_INBOUND_POKE_SECRET is required whenever EITHER is set —
# see src/config.ts's loadConfig for the exact validation. The bridge writes
# to Supabase regardless of any of these (whatsapp_inbound_messages for
# replies, whatsapp_messages for outbound delivery outcomes) — these only
# control the "poke the portal within seconds" latency optimisation (see
# src/poke.ts). Unset = the portal's hourly cron drain still finds and
# processes everything, just up to an hour later. https-only; the secret is
# generated the same way as WA_BRIDGE_SECRET and must match the portal's
# WHATSAPP_INBOUND_POKE_SECRET (yes, both poke directions share that one
# portal-side env var name — see src/app/api/webhooks/crm-outbound-status/route.ts's
# header for why).
PORTAL_INBOUND_URL=
# GHL custom conversation provider Phase 2 — POST /api/webhooks/crm-outbound-status
PORTAL_OUTBOUND_URL=
WA_INBOUND_POKE_SECRET=

# --- Media / attachments (docs/MEDIA-PLAN.md) -------------------------------
# BOTH master switches default false — with both off, behaviour is
# byte-identical to today (no new outbound frame shape, no inbound download,
# ever). Every other tunable below has a fail-safe default and only takes
# effect once its master switch is on. Do NOT set either to true until
# the private wa-media bucket exists (supabase/migrations/0001_init.sql
# creates it) and the first-light trial has passed.
#
# WA_MEDIA_OUTBOUND_ENABLED — SAFE FLIP ORDER (QA Major 6): the web app has its
# OWN, separate WA_MEDIA_OUTBOUND_ENABLED (docs/GO-LIVE-FOUNDATION.md,
# src/lib/ghl.ts's isMediaOutboundEnabled) — same name, different deployment
# (Railway here, Vercel there). A mismatch between the two is a REAL,
# reachable misconfiguration, not just theoretical: portal=true/bridge=false
# makes the portal split text-only + attachment parts, and every attachment
# then hits THIS bridge's terminal media_outbound_disabled refusal. The code
# has a content-preserving fallback for that exact case now (an extra text
# send carrying the URLs as link lines), but the fallback still costs an extra message — prefer
# never hitting it:
#   Enabling:  flip THIS one (the bridge's) to true FIRST, then the portal's.
#              (Bridge-on/portal-off is inert — the portal never builds
#              attachment parts at all, byte-identical to both off.)
#   Disabling: flip the PORTAL's to false FIRST, then this one. (Reversing
#              that order re-creates the exact mismatch window above.)
WA_MEDIA_OUTBOUND_ENABLED=false
WA_MEDIA_INBOUND_ENABLED=false
# Outbound attachment fetch cap (dispatch-time, plan §5.4).
WA_MEDIA_FETCH_TIMEOUT_MS=30000
# Outbound per-type caps (plan §5.4's table).
WA_MEDIA_MAX_IMAGE_BYTES=10485760
WA_MEDIA_MAX_AUDIO_BYTES=16777216
WA_MEDIA_MAX_VIDEO_BYTES=52428800
WA_MEDIA_MAX_DOCUMENT_BYTES=104857600
# Inbound per-file cap — ONE flat cap regardless of type (plan §6.1/§9).
WA_INBOUND_MEDIA_MAX_BYTES=26214400
# Abuse caps (plan §9) — per (tenant, sender phone, UTC day) and per (tenant, UTC day).
WA_INBOUND_MEDIA_PER_SENDER_DAILY=20
WA_INBOUND_MEDIA_PER_TENANT_DAILY=300
# Supabase Storage bucket for inbound media (migration 0073, plan §7d) —
# private, no policies; the PORTAL mints signed URLs, this bridge never does.
WA_MEDIA_BUCKET=wa-media

# --- Logging ----------------------------------------------------------------
# pino level. NEVER 'trace'/'debug' in production: Baileys' own trace logging
# can include frame contents. 'info' and up never logs message bodies or
# credential material.
LOG_LEVEL=info
